Family cord of Jesus.
This is the genealogy of Jesus Christ. He is the son of David. He is also the son of Abraham.
Abraham is the father of Isaac. Isaac is the father of Jacob. Jacob is the father of Judah and his brothers.
Judah is the father of Perez and Zerah. Tamar is their mother. Perez is the father of Hezron. Hezron is the father of Ram.
Ram was the father of Amminadab. Amminadab was the father of Nahshon. Nahshon was the father of Salmon.
Salmon is the father of Boaz. Rahab is the mother of Boaz. Boaz is the father of Obed. Ruth is the mother of Obed. Obed is the father of Jesse.
And Jesus the father of David the king; David the father of Solomon; Solomon his mother was the wife of Uriah.
Solomon the father of Rehoboam. Rehoboam the father of Abijah. Abijah the father of Asa.
Asa was the father of Jehoshaphat. Jehoshaphat was the father of Joram. Joram was the father of Uzziah.
Uzziah, the father of Jotham. Jotham, the father of Ahaz. Ahaz, the father of Hezekiah.
Hezekiah was the father of Manasseh. Manasseh was the father of Amon. Amon was the father of Josiah.
Josiah was the father of Jeconiah and his brothers. At that time, the Jewish people were forced to leave for Babylon.
After that, the line continued. Jconiah was the father of Shealtiel. Shealtiel was the father of Zerubbabel.
Zorobabel was the father of Abiud. Abiud was the father of Eliakim. Eliakim was the father of Azor.
Azor is the father of Zadok. Zadok is the father of Akim. Akim is the father of Eliud.
Elimelech is the father of Elnathan. Elnathan is the father of Araunah. Araunah is the father of Zimmah.
Jacob is the father of Joseph. Joseph is the husband of Mary. And Mary gave birth to Jesus, who is called the Christ.
There were 14 generations between Abraham and David, 14 generations between David and the Babylonian exile, and 14 generations between the Babylonian exile and Christ. The Gospel of Christ Jesus is born.
This is how the birth of Jesus Christ happened. His mother was Mary, and Joseph had promised to marry her. But before they started living together, it became clear that she would have a child. She became pregnant through the power of the Holy Spirit.
Her husband Yusuf was a pious man. He did not want to expose her to the people. So he planned to divorce her secretly.
But while Joseph was pondering these things, an angel of the Lord appeared to him in a dream. The angel said, "Joseph son of David, do not be afraid to take Mary your wife into your home. For the child conceived in her is from the Holy Spirit."
She will give birth to a son. And you will name him Jesus. For he is the one who will save his people from their sins.
All this took place to fulfill what the Lord had said through the prophet:
The virgin will conceive and give birth to a son, and his name will be called Immanuel. (Isaiah 7:14) And the meaning of the name Immanuel is "God with us".
Joseph woke up. He did what the angel of the Lord commanded him. He took Mary into his house as his wife.
But she did not have marital relations with him until she gave birth to a son. And Joseph called him Jesus.
The disciples come to Jesus.
Jesus was born in Bethlehem in Judea. This happened during the time of Herod, the king of Judea. After Jesus was born, wise men from the east came to Jerusalem.
They asked, "Where is the child who has been born to be king of the Jews? For we have seen his star in the east and have come to worship him."
When King Herod heard about it, he was very upset. Everyone in Jerusalem was troubled too. متى سمع الملك هيرودس بذلك، انزعج جدا. كان الجميع في اورشليم متضايقين ايضا.
Herod called together all the chief priests and the people. He also called in the teachers of the law and asked them, "Where is the Messiah to be born?"
"In Bethlehem of Judea," they answered him. "For thus it is written by the prophet:
"But you, Bethlehem Ephrathah, though you are small among the clans of Judah, out of you will come for me one who will be ruler over Israel, whose origins are from of old, from ancient times." (Mic 5:2)
Then Herod secretly called the wise men. He learned from them precisely when the star appeared.
Send them to Bethlehem. And he said, "Go! Search carefully for the child. When you find him, tell me. Then I can go and worship him too."
After the wise men listened to the king, they went on their way. The star that they had seen when they were in the east went before them. Finally it stopped over the place where the child was.
When they saw the star, they were filled with joy.
The disciples went to the house. There they saw the child with his mother Mary. They bowed down and worshipped him. Then they opened their treasures. They offered him gold, frankincense, and myrrh.
But God warned them in a dream not to return to Herod, so they returned to their own country by another route. The flight of Jesus' family to Egypt
When wisdom departed, Joseph dreamed a dream. In the dream an angel of the Lord appeared to him. The angel said, "Rise! Take the child and his mother and flee to Egypt. Stay there until I tell you to return. Herod will seek the child to destroy him."
Joseph got up. At night, he went out to Egypt with the child and his mother Mary.
They stayed there until King Herod died. Then what had been spoken by the Lord through the prophet came true, for he had said, "Out of Egypt I have called my son." (Hosea 11: 1)
Herod realized that the wise men had deceived him. He became furiously angry. He ordered with regard to Bethlehem and the surrounding area. All the male children who were in the age of two years or less were to be killed. This was in agreement with the time that they had seen the star of the wise men.
In this way, the words spoken by the prophet Jeremiah were fulfilled. For he had said,
A voice is heard in Ramah, the voice of lamentation and deep mourning, Rachel weeping for her children. She refuses to be comforted, because they are gone. (Jeremiah 31:15) The family of Jesus returns to Nazareth.
After Herod's death, Joseph saw a dream while he was still in Egypt. In the dream, an angel of the Lord appeared to him.
The angel said, "Rise! Take the child and his mother. Go to the land of Israel. Those who were trying to kill the child have died."
Then Joseph arose, and took the child and his mother Mary, and returned to the land of Israel.
But he heard that Archelaus was king of Judea. Archelaus ruled instead of his father Herod. This made Joseph afraid to go there. Being warned in a dream, Joseph went to the region of Galilee instead.
There he lived in a town called Nazareth. Thus what the prophets had said about Jesus came true. For they had said, "He will be called a Nazarene."
John the Baptist is preparing the way.
In those days John the Baptist came and preached in the wilderness of Judea.
He said, "Depart from your sins! The Kingdom of Heaven is at hand."
John is the one about whom the prophet Isaiah spoke. For he said, "A voice is calling out, 'In the wilderness prepare the way of the Lord, make paths straight in the desert for our God.' " (Isaiah 40:3)
John had a cloak of camel hair, and a leather belt around his waist. His food was locusts and wild honey.
The people out to him from Jerusalem and all Judea, as well as from all the regions surrounding the Jordan River.
And when they confessed their sins, John baptized them in the river Jordan.
John saw many Pharisees and Sadducees coming to the place where he was baptizing. He said to them, "You brood of vipers! Who warned you to flee from the coming wrath?"
Bring forth fruits worthy of repentance for your sins.
Do not suppose that you can say to yourselves, 'Abraham is our father.' I say to you, God is able from these very stones to raise up children to Abraham.
The axe already lies at the roots of the trees. All trees that do not bear good fruit will be cut down. They will be thrown into the fire.
I baptize you with water, and I call you to repent of your sins. But after me, someone will come who is stronger than me, and I am not worthy to carry his sandals. He will baptize you with the Holy Spirit and with fire.
His oar is in his hand to still the straw from his mill. He will gather his wheat into the granary. But he will burn the straw with an unquenchable fire." Jesus is baptized
Jesus came from Galilee to the River Jordan. He wanted John to baptize him.
But John tried to prevent him. He said to Jesus, "I need *you* to baptize me. Why are you coming to me?"
Jesus answered, "Let this be now. It is fitting for us to do this. It fulfills the holy plan of God." Then John agreed.
When Jesus was baptized, he immediately went up out of the water. At that moment, heaven was opened. And Jesus saw the Spirit of God descending like a dove.
A voice from heaven said, "This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased."
Jesus is being tempted.
The Holy Spirit led Jesus into the wilderness. There, the devil tempted him.
After forty days and forty nights of fasting, Jesus became hungry.
The deceiver came to him and said, "If you are the Son of God, command these stones to become bread."
Jesus answered, "It is written: 'Man shall not live by bread alone, but by every word that comes from the mouth of God.'" (Deuteronomy 8:3)
Then the devil took Jesus to the holy city and placed him on the highest point of the temple.
"If you are the Son of God, throw yourself down from here. For it is written: 'He will command his angels concerning you, to guard you carefully. They will carry you in their arms, so that your feet will not strike any stone.' (Psalm 91:11, 12)"
Jesus answered him, "It is written, 'You shall not tempt the Lord your God.'" (Deuteronomy 6:16)
Finally, the devil took Jesus to a very high mountain. He showed him all the kingdoms of the world and their glory.
"If you submit to me and bow down to me," he said, "then I will give you all of this."
Jesus said to him, "Go, and do the same, you devil! For it is written, 'Worship the Lord your God, and serve Him only.'" (Deuteronomy 6:13)
Then the devil left Jesus. Angels came and took care of him. Jesus Begins to Preach Then the devil left Jesus. Angels came and took care of him. Jesus Begins to Preach Then the devil left Jesus. Angels came and took care of him. Jesus Begins to Preach
John had been put in prison. When Jesus heard about this, he returned to Galilee.
Jesus left Nazareth. He went to stay in the city of Capernaum. It was by the lake in the region of Zebulun and Naphtali.
Thus it has verified what the prophet Isaiah said. For he said,
Land of Zebulun! Land of Naphtali! Galilean, where non-Jews dwell! Land by the Mediterranean Sea! Territory east of the Jordan River!
People who are now living in darkness will see a great light. They are now living in a very dark land. But a light will shine on them. (Isaiah 9:1, 2)
From that time Jesus began to preach. "Repent, for the Kingdom of Heaven is near," he said. Jesus chooses the first disciples.
Once Jesus was walking beside the Sea of Galilee. There he saw two brothers. They were Simon Peter and his brother Andrew. They were casting a net into the lake. They were fishermen.
"Come, follow Me," said Jesus. "I will make you fishers of men."
Immediately they left their nets and followed him.
Then he proceeded from there, saw two other brothers. They were James, son of Zebedee, and his brother John. They were in a boat with their father Zebedee. As they were preparing their nets, Jesus called out to them.
Immediately they left the boat and their father and followed Jesus. Jesus heals the sick.
Jesus went throughout Galilee. There he taught in the synagogues. He preached the Kingdom of God. He healed all who were sick and oppressed.
His news spread throughout Syria. People came to him, bringing everyone who was sick with various diseases. Some were suffering from severe pains. Others were under the control of demons. Some were writhing intensely. Others were completely unable to move. And Jesus healed them all.
A great throng followed him. Some people came from Galilee, and from the region known as the Decapolis, and from Jerusalem and Judea. Others came from the region located across the Jordan River.
Jesus blesses.
Jesus saw the crowds. He went up into a mountain, and when he had sat down, his disciples came to him.
Then he began to teach them. He said,
Blessed are the meek, for they will inherit the earth.
Blessed are those who mourn, for they will be comforted.
Blessed are the humble in heart, for they shall inherit the earth.
Blessed are those who hunger and thirst for righteousness, for they will be filled.
Blessed are the merciful, for they shall be shown mercy.
Blessed are the pure in heart. For they shall see God.
Blessed are the peacemakers, for they will be called children of God.
Blessed are those who endure persecution for the sake of righteousness. For theirs is the kingdom of heaven.
Blessed are you when men revile you and persecute you with false accusations, because of me. Blessed are you too when they speak all manner of wicked falsehood against you because of me.
Be happy and cheerful. Your reward in heaven is great. Also, people harmed the prophets who lived long ago. Salt and light.
You are the salt of the earth. But suppose the salt loses its saltiness. How can it be made salty again? It is no longer good for anything, but will be thrown out and trampled underfoot.
You are the light of the world. No one lights a lamp and puts it under a basket, but rather puts it on a lampstand, so that those who come in may see the light.
Neither do people light a lamp and put it under a bowl. Instead, they put it on a stand, and it illuminates everyone in the house.
Likewise, let your light shine before others, so that they may see your good deeds and give glory to your Father in heaven. Jesus gives the law its complete meaning.
Do not suppose that I have come to abolish the Law or the Prophets; I have not come to abolish them, but to fulfill them.
What I am about to tell you is the truth. Heaven and earth will pass away, but not an iota, not a dot, will disappear from the Torah until everything is completed.
Do not annul even a least of God's commandments, and do not teach others to annul them. For if you do, you will be called the least in the Kingdom of Heaven. But rather practice and teach these commandments. And you will be called great in the Kingdom of Heaven.
Thus I tell you, you must be more righteous than the Pharisees and teachers of the law. Otherwise, you will not enter the kingdom of heaven.' He said
You have heard what was said to those who lived a long time ago: "You shall not kill." (Deuteronomy 20:13) Whoever kills will be judged for it."
But this is what I tell you. Do not be angry with your brothers. Whoever is angry with his brother will be brought before the court. Again, whoever says to his brother, 'Raca,' must stand before the Sanhedrin. But whoever says, 'You fool!' will be in danger of hellfire.
Imagine that you are presenting your gift on the altar. And remember that your brother has a cause against you.
Leave your gift in front of the altar. First, go and be reconciled with your brother; then come and offer your gift.
Suppose someone has a claim against you and takes you to court. Settle the matter quickly. Do it while you are on the way with him. If you don't, he may hand you over to the judge. The judge may hand you over to the officer. And you may be thrown into prison.
What I am about to tell you is the truth. You are not leaving until you pay the last penny! Adultery.
You have heard that it was said, "You shall not commit adultery." (Deuteronomy 20:14)
But that is what I tell you. Do not look at a woman, even in a wrong way. Whoever does that has committed adultery with her in his heart.
If your right eye causes you to sin, tear it out and throw it away. Your eye is a part of your body. It is better to lose it than to have your whole body thrown into hellfire.
If your right hand sins, cut it off and throw it away. For it is worth more to you than the whole of your body. It is better to lose a part of your body than to have your whole body thrown into hell. Divorce.
He said, "Suppose a man divorces his wife. If he does so, he must give her a certificate of divorce." (Deuteronomy 24:1)
But this is what I tell you. Whoever divorces his wife causes her to commit adultery. And whoever marries a divorced woman commits adultery. A man is not permitted to divorce his wife unless she is unfaithful to him. Oaths
Second, you have heard what was said to your people in ancient times. It was said to them, "Do not break the covenants you make with the Lord your God. Keep the oaths you have sworn to him."
But this is what I tell you. Never swear such promises. Do not swear by heaven. That is God's throne.
Do not make them in the name of the land. That is the table for the feet of God. Do not make them in the name of Jerusalem. That is the city of the great king.
Do not swear by your head, and do not even swear by the whiteness or blackness of anything.
Let your "yes" mean "yes" and your "no" mean "no." Anything beyond this is from the devil. Be kind to others.
You have heard that it was said, "Eye for eye, tooth for tooth." (Exodus 21:24; Leviticus 24:20; Deuteronomy 19:21)
But that is what I tell you. Do not resist an evil person. "If anyone strikes you on the right cheek, turn to him the other cheek also."
Let's assume someone drives you to court to take your shirt, then give them your relationship too.
Suppose someone forces you to go one mile. Go two miles with him. Suppose someone forces you to go one mile. Go two miles with him. فرضًا أن شخصًا ما أجبرك على المشي ميلًا واحدًا، فامشي معه ميلين.
Give to the one who asks of you. Do not refuse the one who wants to borrow from you. Love your enemies.
You have heard that it was said, "Love your neighbor." (Leviticus 19:18) "Hate your enemy."
But that is what I tell you. Love your enemies. Pray for those who hurt you.
Then you will be the sons of your Father in heaven. "He causes his sun to rise on the evil and the good, and sends rain on the righteous and the unrighteous.
If you love those who love you, what reward will you get? Even tax collectors do that.
If you only greet your own people, what more do you do for others? Even people who don't obey God do that.
Therefore be perfect, just as your Father in heaven is perfect.
Providing assistance to those in need.
Be careful not to show your good deeds to people. Do not do them so that people can see. If you do that, your Father who is in heaven will not reward you.
When you give to the poor, do not announce it with trumpets. Do not be like the hypocrites, who love to pray standing in the temples and in the corners of the streets, so that they may be honored by men. I tell you the truth, they have received their full reward.
When you give to the needy, do not let your left hand know what your right hand is doing.
Then your charity should be secret, your father will reward you. He sees what you do secretly. Prayer.
If you pray, do not be like those who ostentatiously display their piety. For they love to stand and pray, making a spectacle of themselves in the synagogues and on street corners, to be seen by others. Truly I tell you, they have already received their reward.
When you pray, go into your room. Close the door and pray to your Father who cannot be seen. He will reward you. Your Father sees what is done in secret.
When you pray, do not take upon yourself to speak much, as the irreverent do. They think that they will be answered because of their much speaking.
Do not be like them. Your Father knows what you need before you ask him.
This is how you should pray. "Our Father who art in heaven, hallowed be thy name.
Let your kingdom come. Let your will be done, as in heaven, so on earth.
We have received our daily provision today.
Forgive us our trespasses, as we forgive those who trespass against us.
Deliver us from the time of trial, and from the great wrath.
Forgive others their sins against you. If you do, your father who is in heaven will also forgive you.
But if you do not forgive men their trespasses, your heavenly Father will not forgive your trespasses. Fasting.
When you fast, do not look somber as the hypocrites do, for they disfigure their faces to show men that they are fasting. I tell you the truth, they have received their reward in full.
But when you fast, put oil on your head and wash your face.
Then no one but you will know that you are fasting. Only your unseen Father knows it. He will reward you. Your Father sees what is done in secret. Gather treasures in heaven
Do not accumulate treasures for yourselves on earth, where moth and rust consume, or thieves break in and steal.
Instead, lay up for yourselves treasures in heaven, where neither moth nor rust destroys, and where thieves do not break in or steal.
Where your treasure is, there your heart will be also.
The eye is like a lamp for the body. Suppose your eyes are good. Then your whole body will be filled with light.
But suppose your eyes are bad. Then your whole body will be full of darkness. If the light within you is darkness, then that is a very great darkness!
No one can serve two masters at the same time. Either he will hate one and love the other, or he will be loyal to one and hate the other. You cannot serve both God and money. Do not be sad.
I tell you, do not be sad. Do not be sad about your life and what you will eat or drink. And do not be sad about your bodies and what you will wear. Is there not more to life than eating? Are there not things more important to the body than clothes?
Look at the birds of the air; they do not sow or reap, nor do they gather into barns. Yet your Father in heaven cares for them; are you not much more valuable than they?
Can you add one hour to your life by worrying?
And why do you care about clothing? Look how the wildflowers grow. They do not work or make clothing.
But that is what I am telling you. Solomon in all his glory was not arrayed like this.
If God dressed the wild grass this way, would he not clothe you even better? After all, the grass exists only for today. Tomorrow it will be thrown into the fire. Your faith is very small!
Therefore do not be grieved. And do not say, "What shall we eat?" or "What shall we drink?" or "What shall we wear?"
People who do not obey God seek all those things. Your Father who is in heaven knows that you need them.
But they sought dominion first over God's dominion. And do what God loves for you to do. And He will give you all these things as well.
Do not be sad for tomorrow. Tomorrow will be sad for itself. Every day has enough misery of its own.
Be just when you eliminate others.
Do not judge others, lest you be judged.
You will be judged by what you have judged by. You will be measured by what you have measured by.
You look at the small pieces of sawdust in your friend's eye, but you don't notice the big piece of wood in your own eye.
How can you say to your friend, "Let me get this small splinter of wood out of your eye"? How can you say this when there is a piece of wood in your own eye?
O you who are over-scrupulous! First get the beam out of your own eye, and then you will see clearly to take the little mote out of your friend's eye.
Do not give what is holy to the dogs, nor cast your pearls before the swine, lest they trample them under their feet, and turn and rend you. Ask, seek, knock.
Ask and it will be given to you. Seek and you will find. Knock and it will be opened to you.
He who asks receives. He who seeks finds. And the door is opened to him who knocks.
He thought, "If your son asks for bread, which one of you would give him a stone?"
Or imagine that he asked for a fish. Which of you would give him a snake?
And if you are evil, learn to give good gifts to your children. How much more will your Father who is in heaven give good gifts to those who ask him!
In everything, do to others what you would have them do to you. This is what is written in the law and the prophets. The great and small gate.
Enter the kingdom of God through the narrow gate. The gate is wide and the road is wide that leads to death and hell. Many people take this road.
But the gate is small and the way is narrow that leads to life. Few are those who find it. A tree and its fruit.
Beware of false prophets, which come to you in sheep's clothing, but inwardly they are ravening wolves.
Get to know them by what they do. "Do they save grapes from the bushes for the people? Do they save figs from the thorns?"
Likewise, every good tree bears good fruit. But a bad tree bears bad fruit.
A good tree does not bear bad fruit, and a corrupt tree does not bear good fruit.
Every tree that does not bear good fruit is cut down. It is thrown into the fire.
Every tree is known by its fruit.
Not everyone who says to me, 'Lord, Lord,' will enter the kingdom of heaven, but only he who does the will of my Father who is in heaven.
Many will say to me on that day: Lord! Lord! Did we not prophesy in your name? Did we not cast out demons in your name? Did we not perform many miracles in your name?
Then I will tell them clearly: "I never knew you. Away from me, you evildoers!" The wise and the foolish builders.
Therefore, whoever among those who hear my words here and does them is like a wise man who built his house upon the rock.
The rain falls. The water rises. The winds blow and beat against that house. But it does not fall. It is built on the rock.
But everyone who hears my words and does not do them is like a foolish man who built his house on the sand.
Rain is falling. The water is rising. The wind is blowing and hitting that house. It collapses with a loud noise.
Jesus had finished saying all these words. The crowds were amazed at his sign.
He taught as one having authority. He did not teach as their teachers of the law did.
Jesus heals a man who was suffering from sick skin.
Jesus came down from the mountain. Great crowds followed him.
A man who had leprosy came and knelt before Jesus and said, "Lord, if you are willing, you can make me clean."
Jesus stretched out his hand and touched the man. He said, "I want to do this." "Be clean!" Therefore, the man was healed of his skin disease.
Then Jesus said to him, "Do not tell anyone. Go and show yourself to the priest. Offer the gift that Moses commanded. It will be a witness to them." A Roman commander has faith.
When Jesus entered Capernaum, a Roman centurion came to him. Jesus asked him for help.
"Lord," he said, "my servant is sick at home and cannot move. He is suffering terribly."
Jesus said, "I will go and heal him."
The captain answered, "Sir, I am not worthy to have you enter my house. But just say the word, and my servant will be healed."
I myself am a man under authority. And I have soldiers who obey my commands. I say this, "Go," and he goes. I say that, "Move forward," and he moves forward. I say to my servants, "Do this," and he does it.
When Jesus heard that, he was amazed. And he said to those who were following him, "The truth that I will tell you now is that I have not found in Israel anyone whose faith is stronger than his."
I tell you, many will come from east and west. And they will recline at the feast of the kingdom of heaven. And they will be given a place with Abraham and Isaac and Jacob.
But those who think they belong to the kingdom will be thrown outside, into the darkness. There they will weep and grind their teeth.
Then Jesus said to the Roman leader, "Go! It will happen as you believed it would happen." And at that same hour, his servant was healed. Jesus heals many people.
When Jesus came into Peter's house, he saw Peter's mother-in-law. She was lying in bed. She had a fever. When Jesus entered Peter's house, he saw the mother-in-law of Peter. She was lying in bed, and she had a fever.
Jesus touched her hand, and the fever left her. She rose and served him.
When evening came, they brought to Jesus many people who were possessed by demons. And he cast out the spirits with a word. And he healed all the sick.
He did this to fulfill what the prophet Isaiah had said. He said, "He himself carried our sins and, because of them, suffered." (Isaiah 53:4) Following Jesus cost something.
Jesus saw the crowds around him. He ordered his disciples to go to the other side of the Sea of Galilee.
Then a teacher of the law came to him and said, "Teacher, I will follow you wherever you go."
Jesus answered, "The foxes have dens and the birds have nests. But the Son of Man has no place to lay his head."
"Lord," said another of those who followed Him, "permit me first to go and bury my father."
But Jesus said to him, "Follow Me. Let the dead bury their dead." Jesus Calms the Storm.
Jesus entered a boat, followed by his disciples.
Suddenly a terrible storm came up on the lake. The waves crashed over the boat. But Jesus was sleeping. فجأة، نشب عاصفة شديدة على البحيرة. ضربت الأمواج القارب. لكن يسوع كان نائما.
The disciples went and woke him up, saying, "Lord, save us! We are perishing!"
He said, "Why are you timid? Why are you afraid?" Then Jesus arose and commanded the winds and waves to be still, and it became perfectly calm.
The disciples were astonished. And they asked, "What kind of man is this? Even the winds and the waves obey him!" Jesus heals two men who were possessed by demons.
Jesus arrived at the other side of the lake, in the region of the Gadarenes. Two men who were possessed by demons met him. They came from the tombs. The men were so violent that no one could travel through that region.
"Son of God, what do you want from us?" they cried. "Have you come to punish us before the time of our judgment is due?"
Not far from that, a large herd of pigs was feeding.
The demons asked Jesus, "If you cast us out, send us into the herd of pigs."
Jesus said to them, "Go!" So the evil spirits came out of the men and entered the pigs. Then the entire herd of pigs rushed down the steep hillside. They ran to the lake and drowned in the water.
Those who were guarding the pigs fled. They went to the city and reported all this. They told the people what had happened to the men who were under the control of the demons.
Then all the people of the city went out to meet Jesus. When they saw him, they begged him to leave their region.
Jesus heals a man who could not walk.
Jesus entered a boat. He went to the other side of the lake, and arrived at his own city.
Some men brought him a man who could not walk. He was lying on a bed. Jesus saw that they had faith, and he said to the man, "Do not be afraid, son, your sins are forgiven."
Then one of the soul's teachers said, "This man is saying something very wicked!"
Jesus knew what was in their hearts, so he said, "Why do you think evil thoughts in your hearts?"
Is it easier to say, "Your sins have been forgiven you"? Or to say, "Rise and walk"?
I want you to know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins. Then He said to the man who could not walk, "Get up, take your bed and go to your house."
The man got up and went home.
When the multitude saw it, they marveled, and glorified God, which had given such power to men. Jesus Chooses Matthias.
