Jesus sends the twelve disciples.
Jesus called his twelve disciples to come to him. He gave them authority to cast out demons and to heal every disease and weakness.
Here are the names of the twelve apostles. The first of them is Simon Peter and his brother Andrew. Then James the son of Zebedee and his brother John.
After that, Philips and Bartolmaus, as well as Thomas and Matthew the tax collectors. Two others are James the son of Alphaeus and Thaddaeus.
The last two are Simon the Zealot and Judas Iscariot. Judas is the one who will later betray Jesus into the hands of his enemies.
Jesus sent these two after instructing them: "Do not go to the Gentiles," he said, "and do not enter a city of the Samaritans."
Instead, go to the people of Israel. They are like sheep who have gone astray.
Go and preach, saying, 'The kingdom of heaven is at hand.'
Healing the sick. Raising the dead. Cleansing those with leprosy. Driving out demons. You received it freely, so give freely.
Do not take gold or silver or bronze in your belt.
Do not take a suitcase for the trip. Do not take extra clothes, sandals, or walking sticks. The worker should be given what he needs.
When you enter a town or village, look for someone willing to receive you. Stay in that person's home until you leave.
Upon entering the house, greet those who live there.
If that house welcomes you, then bless it with the blessing of peace. If it does not welcome you, then do not bless it.
Some people may not receive you or listen to your words. If they do not, shake the dust from your feet as you leave that house or town.
What I am about to tell you is the truth. On the Day of Judgment, it will be easier for Sodom and Gomorrah than for that city.
I send you out like sheep among wolves, so be wise as serpents and innocent as doves.
