The Farmer's Story
That same day, Jesus left the house and sat by the Sea of Galilee. اليوم نفسه، خرج يسوع من المنزل وجلس عند بحيرة طبرية.
A large crowd gathered around him. So he entered a ship. He sat in it. All the people stood on the shore.
Then he told them many things using stories. He said, "A farmer went out to sow his seeds."
Some seeds fell on the road. Birds came and ate them.
Some seeds fell in rocky places, where there wasn't much soil. The plants grew quickly, because the soil wasn't deep.
When the sun rose, it burned the plants. They dried out because they had no roots.
Others fell among thorns, which pierced them.
Some of the seeds fell on good soil, and they produced a crop that yielded a hundred, sixty or thirty times as much as had been planted.
Let him who has ears hear.
The students came to him. They asked, "Why do you use stories when you talk to people?"
He said, "You have been given the opportunity to understand the secrets of the Kingdom of Heaven. That has not been given to others."
All those who possess that type of knowledge will be given more. In fact, they will have a lot. As for those who do not possess that type of knowledge, even what little they have will be taken from them.
Here is the reason I use stories when I talk to people. I say: "They are looking, but they don't really see. They are listening, but they don't really hear or understand."
In them the words of the prophet Isaiah are fulfilled. He said: "You will indeed hear but never understand; you will indeed see but never perceive."
The hearts of these people have become callous. They barely hear with their ears. They have closed their eyes. Otherwise, perhaps they would see with their eyes. Perhaps they would hear with their ears. Perhaps they would understand with their hearts. Perhaps they would turn back to the Lord, and he would heal them. (Isaiah 6:9,10)
But blessed are the eyes that see what you see, and blessed are the ears that hear what you hear.
What I am going to tell you is the truth. Many prophets and good men have wanted to see what you see. But they did not see. They wanted to hear what you hear. But they did not hear.
Listen up! Here is the meaning of the story of the farmer.
People hear the message of the kingdom, but they do not understand it. Then comes the evil one; he steals what was sown in their hearts. These people are like seed sown along the path.
Others received the seed that fell in rocky places. They are the ones who hear the message and immediately receive it with joy.
But they have no origin. So what are they but deceptors? Quick to believe in affliction or torment which comes because of the message.
Others received the seed that fell among the thorns. These are the ones who hear the Word. But afterward, being choked by the anxieties and riches and pleasures of life, they fail to produce a crop.
And others received the seed that fell into good soil. These are the ones who hear the word and understand it. They are the ones who produce a crop, yielding a hundred, sixty or thirty times more than the one who sowed the seed." The Parable of the Weeds
Jesus happened crowd story another. "Here what like Kingdom of Heaven," he said. "Man planted good seeds in his field."
But while everyone was sleeping, his enemy came. His enemy sowed weeds among the wheat and then went away.
