The servants came to their master. They said, "O master, have you not sown good seeds in your field? From where then have come the weeds?"
"An enemy did this," he answered. "The servants asked him: 'Do you want us to go and remove the weeds?'"
'No,' the landlord answered. 'When you get rid of the weeds, you may get rid of the wheat along with them.'
Let them grow together until the time of harvest. Later I will say to the workers what they ought to do. This is what I will tell them: First they gathered the harmful grass. They tied it in bundles to be burned. Then they gathered the wheat. They brought it into my barns.
Jesus told the crowd another parable. He said, "The kingdom of heaven is like a mustard seed. Someone took the seed and planted it in a field."
It is the smallest of all the seeds that you have. But when it grows, it is the largest of all the garden plants. It becomes a tree. The birds come and rest in its branches.
Jesus told them another parable. He said, "The kingdom of heaven is like leaven," he said, "a woman mixed it into a large quantity of flour. The leaven permeated the whole dough."
Jesus spoke all these things to the crowds in parables. He did not speak to them without a parable.
