Their worship means nothing to me. They teach only human precepts.' (Isaiah 29:13)
Jesus called the crowds to Him. And He said, "Listen and understand."
What enters your mouth does not make you "unclean." Rather, it is what comes out of your mouth that makes you "unclean."
Then the disciples came to him, and asked, "Do you not see that the Pharisees were angered when they heard this?"
Jesus came with the answer: "There are plants my Father in heaven did not plant. They will be pulled up by the roots."
Leave them alone. The Pharisees are blind guides. If a blind man leads another blind man, both will fall into a pit.
Peter said, "Explain this to us."
"Do you still not understand?" Jesus asked them.
Don't you see? All that goes into the mouth, goes into the stomach; and then comes out of the body.
But what comes out of the mouth comes from the heart. These are the things that make you "impure".
Evil thoughts come from the heart. So also murder, adultery, and sexual immorality. Also theft, false testimony, and revealing secrets.
Those are the things that make you 'unclean.' But eating without washing your hands does not make you 'unclean.' " إيمان امرأة من كنعان
Jesus went out from Galilee and entered the region of Tyre and Sidon.
A Canaanite woman who lived near Tyre and Sidon came to him and cried out, "Lord! Son of David! Have mercy on me! My daughter is demon-possessed and she suffers terribly."
Jesus did not say a word. His disciples came near to him. They requested him saying, "Remove her. She is constantly screaming behind us."
Jesus answered, "I was only sent to the lost sheep of Israel."
Then the woman knelt before him. "Master! Help me!" she said.
He said, "It is not right for you to take the children's bread and throw it to their dogs."
"Yes, Lord," she said. "But even the dogs eat the crumbs that fall from their master's table."
Then Jesus answered, "Woman, you have great faith! You will be given what you are asking for." And her daughter was healed at that very moment. Jesus Feeds the Four Thousand → ثم أجاب يسوع، "يا امرأة، إن لك إيمانًا عظيمًا! ستُعطين ما تطلبينه." وفي تلك اللحظة نفسها شُفيت ابنتُها. يسوع يُطعم الأربعة آلاف
Jesus left that place. He walked along the shores of the Sea of Galilee. Then he went up to a mountain and sat down.
Great multitudes came to him. They brought the blind and the lame, the mute and the paralyzed, and many others. They laid them at his feet, and he healed them.
The people marveled. Those who were speechless spoke. The disabled were healed. Those who could not walk walked. Those who were blind saw. So the people praised the God of Israel.
Then Jesus called his disciples to him. He said, "I have compassion for these people. They have already been with me three days, and they have nothing to eat. I do not want to send them away hungry, for they might faint on the way home."
His disciples answered and said, "There is nothing here, and from where should we get bread to satisfy such a large crowd?"
"How many loaves of bread do you have?" Jesus asked. "Seven," they replied, "and a few small fish."
Jesus said to the crowd to sit on the ground.
Taking the seven loaves of bread and the fish, He gave thanks. Then He broke them and gave them to the disciples, and the disciples distributed them to the people.
All of them ate and were satisfied. After that, the disciples collected seven baskets of leftover pieces.
The number of men who ate was four thousand. Women and children also ate.
After Jesus had sent the crowds away, he went up into a boat, and went into the region near Magadan.
Jesus is asked to show a miraculous sign.
The Pharisees and Sadducees came to test Jesus. They asked him to show them a miraculous sign from heaven.
He said, "In the evening you look at the sky. You say, 'It will be fine weather. The sky is red.'"
And in the morning she says, "Today will be windy. The sky is red and cloudy." You know what you see in the sky means. But you do not understand the signs of what is happening now.
A corrupt and unbelieving people, they seek a sign or wonder. But no sign will be given them except the sign of Jonah. Then Jesus left them and went away. The yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
The pupils crossed over to the other side of the lake. They had forgotten to take bread.
"Beware," Jesus said to them. "Beware of the leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
The students are talking to each other about this matter. They said, "He is only saying this because we did not bring bread."
Jesus, knowing what they were saying, said to them, "Why has your faith grown weak? Why are you talking among yourselves about having no bread?"
Haven't you understood yet? Don't you remember the five loaves for the five thousand? Don't you remember how many baskets of fragments you collected?
Do you not remember the seven loaves for the four hundred? Do you not remember how many baskets of fragments you collected?
How can you not understand? I was not speaking to you about bread. But beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
Then the disciples understood that Jesus was not telling them to be careful about the wine used in the bread. Rather, He was warning them about what the Pharisees and Sadducees were teaching. Peter says that Jesus is the Christ.
Jesus went to the district of Caesarea Philippi. There he asked his disciples, "Who do people say that the Son of Man is?"
They said: "Some of them say that this is John the Baptist, and some of them say that this is Elijah, and some of them say that this is Jeremiah, or one of the prophets."
"And you?" he asked. "What do you say about me?"
Simon Peter answered, "You are the Christ. You are the Son of the living God."
Jesus answered, "Blessed are you, Simon son of Jonah! For flesh and blood has not revealed this to you, but my Father who is in heaven. And I tell you, you are Peter, and on this rock I will build my church, and the gates of Hades will not overcome it."
Thus I tell you, you are Peter, and upon this rock I will build my church. The gates of hell shall not prevail against it.
I will give you the keys to the kingdom of heaven. What you bind on earth will be bound in heaven, and what you loose on earth will be loosed in heaven.
Then Jesus warned his disciples not to tell anyone that he was the Christ. Jesus speaks about his upcoming death.
From that time Jesus began to explain to his disciples what would happen to him. He told them that he must go to Jerusalem. There he must suffer many things from the elders, the chief priests, and the teachers of the law. He must be killed, and on the third day he must rise from the dead.
Peter took Jesus aside and began to rebuke him. "Never, Lord!" he said. "This will never happen to you!"
"Be off, Satan!" said Jesus to Peter. "You are an offense to me. For you are not on my side, but are for the interests of men."
Then Jesus spoke with his disciples. He said, "If anyone wants to follow me, he must deny himself, take up his cross, and follow me."
If he wants to save his life, he will lose it. But if he loses his life for me, he will find it.
What good is it if a person gains the whole world but loses their own soul? Or what can anyone trade their own self for?
The Son of Man will come in the glory of His Father with His angels, and He will repay each one according to his works.
What I am now going to tell you is the truth. Some of those who are standing here will not taste death before they see the Son of Man coming in His kingdom.
Jesus changes in appearance.
After six days, Jesus took Peter, John, and James, the brother of John, and went up on a high mountain. There they were alone.
Before them, his appearance changed. His face shone like the sun. His clothes became white as light.
Then Moses and Elijah appeared before them. Moses and Elijah were talking with Jesus.
Peter said to Jesus, "Lord, it is good for us to be here. If you wish, I will make three dwellings for you - one for you, one for Moses, and one for Elijah."
And while Peter was still speaking, a bright cloud enveloped them. A voice from the cloud said, "This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased. Listen to him!"
When the disciples heard this, they were exceedingly afraid. They fell on their faces to the ground.
But Jesus came and touched them, saying, "Arise, and do not be afraid."
When they looked up, they saw no one except Jesus.
They went down the mountain. And while going down, Jesus instructed them what they should do. And he said, "Do not tell anyone what you have seen. Wait until the Son of Man has risen from the dead."
The disciples asked him, "Why do the writers of the law say that Ali must come first?"
Jesus answered, "It is true. Elijah must come and restore all things again."
But I tell you that Elijah has come already, and they did not recognize him, but did to him whatever they pleased. Likewise, they will also make the Son of Man suffer.
Then the disciples understood that Jesus was speaking to them about John the Baptist. Jesus heals a boy in whom was a demon.
As they came near to the crowd, a man ran up to Jesus. He knelt down before him.
"Oh Lord," he said, "have mercy on my son. He is convulsing severely and suffering greatly. He often falls into the fire or into the water."
I brought him to your disciples, but they could not heal him.
"O ye wicked and evil generation!" Jesus answered. "How long shall I be with you? How long shall I suffer you? Bring him here to me."
Jesus commanded the demon to come out of the boy, and it came out. He was healed at that moment.
Then the disciples came to Jesus privately. And they asked, "Why could we not cast out this demon?"
He said, "Because your faith is very small. What I am going to tell you is the truth. If your faith is as small as a mustard seed, it is enough. You can say to this mountain, 'Be removed and cast into the sea.' And it will be done. Nothing will be impossible for you."
